**Diary — 2026-03-14 — Porting the CUDA engine to HIP on gfx906**

Today I moved the core CUDA engine onto HIP for gfx906. The goal was not a clean rewrite, just a working port: same kernels, same memory model, same pipeline, running on ROCm.

The first pass was mechanical. I replaced CUDA runtime calls with HIP equivalents:

```cpp
cudaMalloc      -> hipMalloc
cudaFree        -> hipFree
cudaMemcpyAsync -> hipMemcpyAsync
cudaStream_t    -> hipStream_t
cudaEvent_t     -> hipEvent_t
cudaDeviceSynchronize -> hipDeviceSynchronize
```

Kernel declarations were mostly unchanged:

```cpp
__global__ void engine_kernel(...)
__shared__ float smem[256];
```

The annoying part was not the API rename. It was the assumptions baked into the CUDA engine.

The engine used a lot of 32-lane warp assumptions. On gfx906, the hardware wavefront is 64 lanes. That broke a few reduction kernels. I had a warp reduction written like this:

```cpp
for (int offset = 16; offset > 0; offset >>= 1)
    val += __shfl_down_sync(0xffffffff, val, offset);
```

On CUDA, that is fine for 32 lanes. On gfx906, `__shfl_down_sync` works, but the lane semantics are 64-lane. I changed the reduction to use HIP’s cooperative groups where available:

```cpp
#include <hip/hip_cooperative_groups.h>
namespace cg = cooperative_groups;

__global__ void reduce_kernel(float* out, const float* in, int n) {
    cg::thread_block block = cg::this_thread_block();
    cg::thread_block_tile<32> tile = cg::tiled_partition<32>(block);

    float val = in[block.thread_rank()];

    for (int i = 16; i > 0; i >>= 1)
        val += tile.shfl_down(val, i);

    if (tile.thread_rank() == 0)
        atomicAdd(out, val);
}
```

